<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · mountain</p><h1 class="enc-title">Bavarian Alps</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Bavarian <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alps">Alps</a> are the part of the Northern Limestone Alps along <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bavaria">Bavaria</a>'s border with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austria">Austria</a>, including the Zugspitze, at 2,962 metres the highest mountain in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a>. Resorts such as Garmisch-Partenkirchen and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/berchtesgaden">Berchtesgaden</a> lie among them, as do Ludwig II's castles of Neuschwanstein and Linderhof. From the nineteenth century they became one of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>'s favourite holiday regions.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/bavarian-alps.jpg" alt="Bavarian Alps" width="320" height="111" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Bernd Reuschenberg Original uploader was Berreu at de.wikipedia · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Bayerische_Alpen.JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
